t that will  change quickly when he flips this cutoff  lever then the three and a half litre  really shows what it's made of  kristef says that classic coach work  reached its zenith in the 1930s back  then each luxury car was still delivered  directly to the coachbuilder as a  rolling chassis complete with engine  radiator and gearbox the coach builder  then created a custom body based on the  clients wishes this three and a half  liter prophets from Bentley slim  radiator grille which looks sporty and  more elegant than the clunky ones made  for rolls-royce it also made this  aerodynamic design possible christophe  thinks the wonderfully streamlined rear  end is an absolute highlight this model  called the Aero saloon was designed by  English aircraft engineer sir Roy fed  just three were built by the coach  builder park board in Williston making  it a real rarity cavitate over the  course of the 1930s rolls-royce took  over Park Ward which made bodies for  close to half of all the Darby Bentley's  produced the coach builder fashion the  open t'rar the sedan kakou pay and this  version the sports saloon each vehicle  is special but not really one-of-a-kind  like most coach builders Park Ward  created standard styles that had  modified only slightly to suit clients  wishes christophe says that under the  hood you'll find a prime example of  engine tuning 1930 style the super  reliable straight-6 engine has just  under 3.7 liters of capacity to improve  performance the Bentley version had a  higher compression ratio a sporty-er  camshaft profile and a cross flow  cylinder head which is fed by these two  su carburettors this combination  delivers a full 110 horsepower which was  quite something in 1935 and if that  wasn't enough starting in 1936 buyers  could order the brand-new four and a  quarter liter engine which produced a  whopping 130 horsepower  [Music]  he says you have to develop a feel for  shifting gears double de-clutch abit  employ a soft touch and you can switch  gears without stripping them but when  you get it going the car gets 150  kilometers an hour and is a good travel  companion even today no joke some  Bentley enthusiasts even drove around  the world in 80 days in a three and a  half liter Christophe says in its day  the three and a half liter was described  as combining the power of an express  train with a tranquility of a sailboat  today you might say it combines the  reliability and sportiness of a Bentley  with the uncompromising quality of a  Rolls and he has the numbers to prove it  of the 2422 Darby Bentley's ever built  some 70% are still around today that  alone makes the car a classic but  there's more as a remodeled Rolls Royce  this Bentley is one of the first  representatives of what's now known as  platform strategy  so for Christophe the Bentley three and  a half liter is definitely a milestone  in automotive history in the post-war  era Rolls Royce took badge engineering  to the extreme except for the emblems  and hood ornaments most Bentley's were  exact replicas of rolls-royce models  that only changed after the Volkswagen  Group acquired Bentley through its  purchase of rolls-royce motors in 1998  now Bentley is once again creating its  own vehicles and giving rolls-royce now  owned by BMW a run for its money  [Music]
 

